There are several types of phishing attacks [6] which depend on the targeted attacker. SMS phishing is also called smashing. Smashing is mostly done by two methods: first is sending SMS to victims pretending to be a trusted authority like a bank etc and collecting secret information. The second method is sending an SMS that has malware or a link to malware. Vishing is a type of phishing attack using voice calls. Email phishing is mostly used by the attacker due to its wide use and the unknown geographical location of the sender. Phishing is also launched by an attacker by creating a fake website which is the same as a real site. Malware is software that acts as if it is useful but it can harm a computer or collect credentials. Keyloggers record computer users' every key stack. In session hijacking the attacker makes the user create a session ie informs the user that his/her account is blocked, the user checks their login to the bank application then the session starts and the attacker uses the user’s session id or key to collect credentials. Web trojan is a pop-up over the screen. The user thinks that he/she is entering credentials on the website but they are entering it in the attacker pop-up window. Pharming installs a code on the victim's computer which generates traffic on a website without the victim's knowledge. Some phishing solutions are available. One is phishing prevention and the other is phishing detection [7]. Phishing prevention provides an extra security layer via twofactor authentications which works to confirm the identity of the user before giving access to an account on the website. Phishing detection is done by user awareness and software detection. Software detecting techniques are black and white listing, heuristic detection, visual similarity detection, and machine learning [4]. The most widely used anti-phishing solution is a blacklist system but the queries of the blacklist

Phishing websites are distinguished from the legitimate website using features of the website URL. Some features according to Rishikesh Mahajan and Irfan Siddavatam [2] are the presence of IP address and @ symbol in URL, numbers of dots in Hostname, prefix/suffix in the domain, URL redirection, HTTPS in URL, Tiny URL, length of hostname, presence of sensitive words in URL, number of slashes in URL, presence of Unicode in URL, SSL certificate age, anchor URL, iframe, website ranking. In paper [3] a lot of machine learning classifiers are tested on a phishing detection dataset containing 5000 phishing web pages and legitimate web pages. In paper [4], they proposed a methodology that combines a robust classifier SVM algorithm and KNN algorithm that is effective for noisy data. The accuracy of this methodology is 90.04%. A hybrid ensemble feature selection (HEFS) is proposed in paper [5] for phishing website detection based on machine learning algorithms. A cumulative distribution gradient algorithm CDF-g is applied for the primary feature set. Then, the second feature set is extracted using the perturbation ensemble function. HEFS results in 94.6% accuracy to detect phishing attributes. The system presented in the system [6] was made twofold, at first random forest classifier applied and then obtained results integrated with the nearest neighbour algorithm. The accuracy of the RF_NN model is 93.41% on the available dataset. A stacking model proposed using URL and HTML features [7] for phishing webpages detection. LightGBM, XGradientBoost, and Gradient boosted decision trees combined to develop a stacking model. The system uses two datasets and 97.3% accuracy is provided. A comparison of the stacking model and supervised learning to detect phishing is done in this paper [8]. Diverse ML algorithms are analysed including (NN, RF, bagging, Naïve Bayes, k-nearest neighbour, and support vector machine,). Two stacking models are combined Stacking 1 (RF, NN, Bagging) and Stacking 2 (CNN, RF, Bagging) to improve the accuracy of classification. Stacking 1 achieved 97.4% accuracy. The

