Anti-phishing solutions include phishing prevention and detection. Phishing protection is provided by two-factor authentication, which verifies a user's identity before granting access to an account on a website. User knowledge and device identification are used to detect phishing. User awareness refers to educating users about URLs so that they can recognise phishing websites. Black and white listing, heuristic detection [13], visual similarity detection, and machine learning are examples of software detection techniques. The most commonly used anti-phishing approach is the blacklist method, but blacklist system queries use a database of documented phishing URLs, so newly launched phishing URLs are not detected. A machine learning solution to anti-phishing is more intelligent. Hidden patterns in datasets are detected by machine learning algorithms. A classification algorithm is trained using such features that can differentiate a phishing website from a legitimate one in machine learning-based techniques.

