The traditional methods for detecting phishing attacks are ineffective, with only around 20% of phishing attacks being detected. Even on small datasets, machine learning methods produce better performance, but at a cost of scalability and time. Heuristic methods for detecting phishing have a high rate of false positives. To protect against phishing attacks, users must exercise caution. Apart from informing clients about secure surfing, user interface enhancements such as dynamic alerts and, as a result, the detection of suspicious emails, can be made. While IoT gadgets have access to classified resources, their features and security architectures are not yet mature, making them an extremely obvious target for attackers. More people are turning to the internet to complete their daily tasks. As a result, providing a robust phishing attack detection solution with improved response time and accuracy has become increasingly important. Researchers are paying more attention to this research domain due to a rise in the number of phishing attacks linked to Covid-19, as well as attacks on online communication tools.
An all-encompassing phishing site attack detection solution could be built in the future to recognise and block malicious websites without the involvement of the user. If a website requests login details or confidential information, a system or smart web plug-in solution can verify the site's legitimacy and notify the owner (organisation, company, etc.) in advance. Checking the health of web pages while users are browsing has become a necessity, and scalable and reliable solutions are needed.
5. Conclusion
The growth of phishing attacks is huge due to the Covid-19 because everything is online and the detection of these attacks is very low. With the rise in the digital economy, the prevalence of online fraud is rising. This means that we must work more on the detection of phishing attacks. Machine learning is one of the good approaches for detecting phishing attacks. A lot of anti-phishing solutions are available: heuristic detection, virtual similarity detection, black and whitelisting, and machine learning. This paper compares various research for each machine learning technique in terms of detecting phishing attacks and discusses the benefits and drawbacks of each methodology. In addition, this paper presents a detailed list of existing phishing attack threats as well as potential research directions in this sphere. The DNN, LSTM, CNN, is the best technique for detection of phishing attacks which achieves the best accuracy on the base of the small dataset and low features for phishing URL detection.
